 ## Browser tests
 
-The tests that drive the portal in a real browser need Playwright and a browser (`pip install playwright`, then `playwright install chromium`) and are skipped without them. They are in `tests/test_portal_browser.py`, `tests/test_portal_lead_pages.py`, three in `tests/test_portal_wording_and_masking.py`, a few in `tests/test_mode_safety.py`, and the layout and look tests (`tests/test_portal_layout.py`, `tests/test_portal_look.py`), which use the same browser helpers. **The checks in `.github/workflows/lint.yml` do not install Playwright, so they do not run the browser tests;** the masking promise P10 rests on them, so run them on your own computer. To see why any test was skipped, run `python -m pytest -q -rs`.
+The tests that drive the portal in a real browser need Playwright (a Python package that controls a browser) and a browser. Without them these tests are skipped, not failed. They are in `tests/test_portal_browser.py`, `tests/test_portal_lead_pages.py`, `tests/test_stage_confirm_browser.py`, three in `tests/test_portal_wording_and_masking.py`, a few in `tests/test_mode_safety.py`, and the layout and look tests (`tests/test_portal_layout.py`, `tests/test_portal_look.py`), which use the same browser helpers. **The checks in `.github/workflows/lint.yml` do not install Playwright, so they do not run the browser tests;** the masking promise P10 rests on them, so run them on your own computer. Playwright is not in the lock files on purpose: its version has to match the browser build that is installed, and that differs from one computer to the next. No other package is needed (the sidebar colour test reads the screenshot with the Python standard library, not Pillow).
+
+Steps that were measured on Linux with Python 3.12, starting from a new `.venv` made with `requirements-dev.txt` (see "How to run the tests"). The computer already had Chromium build 1243 and Node 22 installed, each in its own folder, so the two folders are written as placeholders below:
+
+    python -m pip install playwright==1.63.0
+    export PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH=<folder that holds the browser>
+    export PATH=<folder that holds node>:$PATH           # the tests also run Node
+    python -m pytest -q -rs
+
+* **The Playwright version must match the browser build.** Playwright 1.63.0 looks for Chromium build 1243. The newest version at the time (1.65.1) looks for build 1254, does not find it, and then every browser test is skipped with a "BrowserType.launch: Executable doesn't exist" message. If you have no browser yet, install the newest Playwright and run `playwright install chromium`, which fetches the build that Playwright wants. That route was not measured here.
+* **Where the browser is not in the default place,** set `PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH` to the folder that holds it: the tests start the browser with no path of their own.
+* **What a full run gives (Linux, Python 3.12, this commit, 2465 tests):** without Playwright, 2349 passed and 116 skipped; with Playwright 1.63.0 and Node, 2462 passed and 3 skipped. The 3 skipped are: the secret scan in `tests/test_hygiene.py` (no `gitleaks` program installed), the load measurement (it runs only when `LEAD_DESK_LOAD_N` is set), and one test that runs only on Windows. A full run takes about 15 to 17 minutes on one process.
+* **The 116 skips without Playwright:** 113 are browser tests that need Playwright (22 in `test_mode_safety.py`, 28 in `test_portal_browser.py`, 60 in `test_portal_lead_pages.py`, 3 in `test_portal_wording_and_masking.py`), plus the 3 above.
+* **Why this can differ from an earlier count of 2329 passed and 135 skipped:** that count is what you get when Node is not installed. Without Node on the path, 21 more tests skip (the tests that run the portal's JavaScript under Node: 1 in `test_mode_safety.py`, 2 in `test_portal_lead_pages.py`, 18 in `test_portal_mode.py`; measured on this commit, 134 skipped instead of 113 in those eight files). Those 21 move from passed to skipped, which gives 2328 passed and 137 skipped on this commit. The earlier count differs from that by one test and one skip. Not explained: whether that earlier run had one fewer test (the total then was 2464, now 2465) or had gitleaks installed; the run's log is not kept. If you see a count near 2329/135, run `python -m pytest -q -rs` and look for "node is not installed".
+* The browser file `test_portal_browser.py` alone takes about 20 seconds; the whole set of browser files about 4 minutes.
+
+## Quality checks and what to do when a check fails
+
+The commands for ruff, mypy, bandit and the schema check are listed under "Quality commands" in `contract.md`, and the same commands, in order, are in `.github/workflows/lint.yml` (that file also has the secret scan and the scan for hardcoded literals). Run them from the repository folder with the `.venv` folder switched on.
+
+When a check fails: read the last lines of its output first; they name the file and line. For a test, run it alone (`python -m pytest -q tests/test_name.py::test_name`). A test that did not run is not a pass: to see why tests were skipped, run `python -m pytest -q -rs`, which prints one line per skip reason (for example "could not import 'playwright.sync_api'", "node is not installed", "no gitleaks program on PATH"). To make a missing secret scan a failure instead of a skip, set `DPLD_REQUIRE_SCAN=1` and `GITLEAKS_BIN` to the program's path.
 
